1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,716
Some questions deserve a model far
bigger than this computer can hold.

2
00:00:05,184 --> 00:00:08,213
A seventy billion parameter model is over

3
00:00:08,253 --> 00:00:11,322
forty gigabytes before it answers a word.

4
00:00:11,894 --> 00:00:15,145
Windlass hauls it through memory one layer

5
00:00:15,185 --> 00:00:18,319
at a time, so an ordinary PC can run it.

6
00:00:18,803 --> 00:00:23,317
It is slow on purpose,
and everything you see today runs live.

7
00:00:23,889 --> 00:00:28,725
Our demo model is a small three
billion one, so you can watch it work.

8
00:00:29,475 --> 00:00:31,135
Here is how it works.

9
00:00:31,175 --> 00:00:33,810
Windlass reads a GGUF
model file from your drive.

10
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:37,616
It reads the header first,
never the whole model.

11
00:00:37,656 --> 00:00:41,637
Everything stays on this
one computer, on loopback.

12
00:00:42,337 --> 00:00:44,910
One layer at a time goes into memory.

13
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:49,089
While layer N computes on the CPU,
layer N plus one loads from disk.

14
00:00:49,129 --> 00:00:52,634
The keys and values cache
stays in RAM, so a seventy

15
00:00:52,674 --> 00:00:56,493
billion model needs about two
gigabytes plus that cache.

16
00:00:57,193 --> 00:01:02,265
Fairlead, the model router, treats
Windlass as one more local provider.

17
00:01:02,305 --> 00:01:05,230
The route is deep-local,
reached by name only,

18
00:01:05,270 --> 00:01:07,720
so interactive tools never wait on it.

19
00:01:08,420 --> 00:01:12,148
And the guardrails: loopback only,
no outgoing connections,

20
00:01:12,188 --> 00:01:16,276
no text in the log, and it never
stops in the middle of a reply.

21
00:01:16,853 --> 00:01:19,051
Setup needs no admin
rights and no install.

22
00:01:19,091 --> 00:01:23,843
First, info reads only the model's
header: twenty-eight layers,

23
00:01:23,883 --> 00:01:29,167
three point four gigabytes, Q8 weights,
and the Llama 3 chat template.

24
00:01:29,207 --> 00:01:31,312
Nothing is ever downloaded.

25
00:01:32,012 --> 00:01:35,005
The overview shows the model,
its measured speed,

26
00:01:35,045 --> 00:01:37,295
and the base URL any program can use.

27
00:01:37,335 --> 00:01:41,573
There is no key to paste:
nothing here leaves the computer.

28
00:01:41,613 --> 00:01:45,958
Now a real task: a short watch log,
and a request for two bullets,

29
00:01:45,998 --> 00:01:49,719
with a system prompt that
says answer only from the log.

30
00:01:50,419 --> 00:01:52,084
The reply streams token by token.

31
00:01:52,124 --> 00:01:56,271
Every token costs a pass over all
twenty-eight layers, so it is slow,

32
00:01:56,311 --> 00:02:00,883
about a second a token here, and a
seventy billion model would take minutes.

33
00:02:00,923 --> 00:02:02,278
That is the trade.

34
00:02:02,978 --> 00:02:06,511
When it finishes, the page shows
the first-token time and the counts.

35
00:02:06,551 --> 00:02:11,710
Now the proof of streaming: the model file
is about thirty-two hundred megabytes,

36
00:02:11,750 --> 00:02:14,662
yet the engine's memory
is about one gigabyte,

37
00:02:14,702 --> 00:02:17,976
because only a couple of
layers sit in RAM at once.

38
00:02:18,726 --> 00:02:22,641
These numbers were
measured on this computer,

39
00:02:22,681 --> 00:02:25,893
not estimated: about a second a reply

40
00:02:25,933 --> 00:02:29,145
token in streaming mode, a tenth of a

41
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:33,012
second a prompt token,
the CPU as the limit,

42
00:02:33,052 --> 00:02:35,913
a perplexity score, and a context

43
00:02:35,953 --> 00:02:39,165
recall of four of four planted facts.

44
00:02:39,205 --> 00:02:41,478
Anything never measured shows a dash.

45
00:02:42,178 --> 00:02:45,856
The Playbook page says why
each setting was chosen.

46
00:02:45,896 --> 00:02:51,133
Our cache of zero overrides the
playbook, as the serve log showed.

47
00:02:51,173 --> 00:02:54,998
For scale, the repo's own
stress test measured a seventy

48
00:02:55,038 --> 00:02:58,932
billion model at about eight
point seven seconds a token.

49
00:02:58,972 --> 00:03:00,392
That was not rerun here.

50
00:03:00,642 --> 00:03:03,595
Now Fairlead. We register Windlass as a

51
00:03:03,635 --> 00:03:06,817
local provider, a custom OpenAI-compatible

52
00:03:06,857 --> 00:03:11,805
one, with a timeout of an hour,
because a layer pass can be long.

53
00:03:11,845 --> 00:03:15,425
Discover finds its one model
and its context length.

54
00:03:15,465 --> 00:03:17,261
Fairlead needs no code for this.

55
00:03:17,961 --> 00:03:21,195
Now the real call, through Fairlead's

56
00:03:21,235 --> 00:03:24,202
address, asking for deep-local-3b.

57
00:03:24,242 --> 00:03:29,298
The model on this machine is working
through its layers, so we skip ahead.

58
00:03:29,998 --> 00:03:35,411
The reply is the model's own,
with its token counts and the time it took

59
00:03:35,451 --> 00:03:41,166
through Fairlead: about ninety seconds,
because the model streams from disk.

60
00:03:41,206 --> 00:03:43,443
Trust details. A request with the wrong

61
00:03:43,483 --> 00:03:45,954
Host name, or an Origin
from some web page,

62
00:03:45,994 --> 00:03:50,023
gets a four oh three, so no browser
tab can quietly drive your model.

63
00:03:50,723 --> 00:03:54,499
Now a long reply is running,
and eleven more requests arrive at once.

64
00:03:54,539 --> 00:03:59,981
The queue holds eight, so three are told
the server is busy, a five oh three that

65
00:04:00,021 --> 00:04:02,756
Fairlead treats as a reason to fail over,

66
00:04:02,796 --> 00:04:06,141
and the rest wait their turn,
first in, first out.

67
00:04:06,181 --> 00:04:08,388
Stop cancels the running reply.

68
00:04:08,942 --> 00:04:11,780
The reply stopped and the queue drained.

69
00:04:11,820 --> 00:04:16,220
And the check command proves the
source makes no outgoing connections,

70
00:04:16,260 --> 00:04:21,103
starts no process and loads nothing
dynamically, before it will even compile.

71
00:04:21,143 --> 00:04:27,000
The evidence trail. Fairlead's Activity
shows the request: client Windlass,

72
00:04:27,040 --> 00:04:31,953
route deep-local-3b, served by
the Windlass provider, a hundred

73
00:04:31,993 --> 00:04:37,733
and two tokens in, sixty-nine out,
first token after eighty-nine seconds.

74
00:04:38,145 --> 00:04:43,126
And Windlass's own log shows request ids,
token counts and timings.

75
00:04:43,166 --> 00:04:46,907
It never records what you
asked or what it answered.

76
00:04:47,157 --> 00:04:50,239
Windlass is slow on purpose
and honest about it.

77
00:04:51,031 --> 00:04:54,811
Everything you saw ran live on
this computer, through Fairlead.

78
00:04:55,597 --> 00:04:58,831
Not built yet: GPU offload.

79
00:04:59,599 --> 00:05:03,804
Not measured yet: batching
throughput on real models.

80
00:05:04,504 --> 00:05:09,298
And the seventy billion timings
are the repo's, not rerun today.

81
00:05:10,173 --> 00:05:13,238
Slow, local, and verifiable.
